  {
    "episode_id": 84578,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84578.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 2,
    "turn_text": "The several layers to this. So in context, the first question is why would we negotiate against ourselves? The House has already voted on funding for Israel. We had it in an offset way to pay for it with cuts to the Democrat plan to grow the IRS enormously. And so you would grow it less enormously. And the Senate has taken no action on that. So we're waiting for a counteroffer from the Senate. And it's always a bad practice to negotiate against yourself. So that was my first objection. And then the second one was we haven't fully funded our own country, which was due on October 1st. So before we fund another country, we should fund our country. And then when you look at security crisis, we haven't agreed to defend our country's borders. And so when you say we're going to defend another country's borders, That's problematic. And so if there was bipartisan agreement on a way to secure the border or fund our country, that would be one thing. But when you have a limited amount of negotiating leverage, when you only control the house, then you have to look for those windows to do it. And so moving on to other topics takes the pressure off on the two things that have to get done. We need to fund our country and then we need to secure our border. And I think the last thing is, you know, Israel's a friend and an ally always try to stand with Israel. I certainly support their reaction to the, you know, brutal terrorist attacks on 10 seven. I think they have a right to exist. And that means they have a right to defend themselves and to respond to threats to their very existence. And they're also a fairly affluent country. And so they can afford to buy weapons and they're they are buying weapons in Unfortunately, the Biden administration is limiting the weapons that they can buy, and they're using the tools that they already have as leverage to try to interfere in Israel's plan to defend themselves against Hamas. And so when we give them even more money, it seems like there would be even more tools for the Biden administration to use as leverage actually against Israel. And so I think that sometimes it might not be good to give the Biden administration more tools in order to impose Washington's will on Israel.",
