  {
    "episode_id": 84578,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84578.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 7,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, and that's an important question because the existing authorization of military force goes back to post 9-11, right? And so we should expire that authorization for military force. and create a much more narrow authorization against named terrorist organizations. We should be very specific about how we do that. And it's clear that the Houthis are, in fact, using terror tactics, right? And they're using these attacks against really random ships. They can say, oh, they're tied to Israel. The only thing that's really tied to Israel is the timing of when they started doing this they're tapping ships that they can reach commercial ships that they can reach through the narrow of passageway along the Red Sea. And they're doing that basically to try to force the negotiation about where their claims to land in Yemen. And so they've had a long civil war there and they want some sort of outside intervention to try to do that allegedly. And it could be that they just want chaos because Iran is funding it. They're supplying the weapons. And Iran certainly is the biggest destabilizing force in the Middle East. And so I think if you look at what are the Houthis doing, you would say, yeah, we can target that. And in order to target that, we should put some limiting factors on that. So if you allowed an unlimited range of options, some people would view that as a license to deploy brigade combat teams and occupy all the land and Yemen and drive our troops down the same supply routes every day and act surprised they got ambushed. Not that that crazy idea was ever tried before, like Iraq, right? That was a terrible approach to that. Unfortunately, our country took too long to realize that was a terrible approach before we changed course. So I think you have to have limiting factors. And to me, what that looks like is you can use air power to go after people that are launching a tax against peaceful shipping vessels, shipping lanes, and you can use persistent surveillance. And I think we should be working with Saudi Arabia to do that. It costs a lot more to operate naval aviation and military aviation and to operate ground-based stuff out of Saudi Arabia and to provide the surveillance. And if we've got the surveillance over top of the areas that the Houthis are operating out of, We can find the targets and then we can take those targets out. And I think that's something that I would authorize given the current situation and we should have that debate and do it and then we should have limiting factors on duration and escalation paths.",
