    "turn_text": "were fighting a civil war and it was over land and governance of Yemen, frankly, right? What is the role of the Houthis in the government there in which land did the various factions control. In our case, I think the limiting factor is we have peaceful navigable waterways and we should do things to protect free trade and shipping and peaceful there. Now the reality is this comes in the context of a lot of destabilizing factors in the Middle East. Certainly Iran is a big player in these destabilizing factors. But if you go from Yemen to Turkey, from Iran to the Mediterranean Sea, pretty much everything in that region is in a bit of turmoil. And if you look at the illicit finance, gutter is certainly a key part of this as well. So if I could do anything, I would probably got our own State Department and leave them in a position to seek a peaceful resolution to it versus war. I think we could probably get in fewer wars without our State Department as it's currently staffed and structured. But ideally, you would have a State Department that is actually focused on de-escalation of this conflict and, frankly, limiting the spread of this conflict. and also mining the sovereignty of the countries. So the Abraham Accords, which the Trump administration facilitated the negotiation of, were very successful. And the reason that the Iran is trying to blow this whole situation up is because it was very successful. You started having good dialogue with not just Saudi Arabia, but other Sunni countries in the Middle East. and Iran's Shia government views that as destabilizing for their own interests there, and they're in conflict. And so they basically want to scuttle the Abraham Accords with these kinds of attacks, and we shouldn't reward that. We should say, no, not only are the Abraham Accords in place, they're going to expand, and we're going to have a peaceful resolution in this area, despite your best efforts.",
