  {
    "episode_id": 84578,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84578.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 12,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, it is weird. Once I got elected to Congress, I was really surprised by the number of people whose main job really is to vote on issues, how much effort they go to to avoid voting. And the big thing is, once you go and vote, people can hold you accountable for how you vote. And people want to avoid accountability and responsibility. It's the same. Like I had an amendment in the summer that said, before we fund something, $300 million that was going to go to Ukraine, just define the mission. Tell us the mission. And we only get 129 votes, but at least we forced a vote. It didn't pass, obviously. But how do you hold the State Department accountable for success or failure if they don't have to define the mission that they're trying to achieve? paint the target on after you've taken the shot, you can make it look pretty good or pretty bad depending on what your objective is, right? And so we have to define that up front and frankly unless you tell me what the mission is, I don't truly know what resources you need. And so there tends to be this idea that Congress is supposed to just shut up and cut the checks and then let the executive branch do that. That's not our system of government. But a lot of this has gone on under that sort of false premise. I think the other thing is because the public doesn't trust Congress, in part because of the past behavior here, The public largely supports a strong executive solution when it comes to threats to national security. They know that in general somebody can at least be decisive. Right now is a window of opportunity because they know that in general the Biden administration is very weak and we're being taken advantage of on every front. They know that even the exit out of Afghanistan was terrible. You don't have to have served in the military to know that first you get the civilians out and then you take the military out. The Biden administration did that the wrong way. And that is their issue. The Trump administration didn't commit to how to get out of Afghanistan. They just said, we're going to get out. And so I think the opportunity is for Congress to update our authorizations for military force to expire everything and to start anew And one of the limiting factors we should do is every Congress should have to vote, right? You force a vote every Congress, and that's how you know the country is still committed to this. They're committed to it in a different way, or they're no longer committed to it. And a lot of people say, well, that's a date certain to fail. No, why would you assume that it's going to fail? The default is Congress always says, yeah, let's have more wars in more places so far. It's rare that Congress turns off anything. They just keep cutting the checks. But the reality is you should have a way that you know every Congress is going to at least vote on this. So nothing should extend past each Congress. So we're in the 118th. If we want something that like the War on Terror probably will continue for a long time. Well, the 119th, if they want to stay waging it the same way, they have to vote. And if they want to change something, that vote is the leverage to force some change. And those are features that bipartisan coalition do agree on. And hopefully, as we review those, we can actually get that to become the law.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
