  {
    "episode_id": 84578,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84578.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 14,
    "turn_text": "I think it's a good question. It is interesting that some of our biggest warharks aren't people who served. And frankly, I suspect wouldn't serve. But nevertheless, lots of people who did serve their default position is to support more engagement than I have supported. And I'll tell you one of the things that really is top of mind for me is when I served in 75th Ranger Regiment, I had one of my soldiers who had served in Ranger Regiment when they were involved in Moby-Dishu Somalia. And after six Rangers got killed, there were others that were killed there too, but six people from his unit were killed, the United States left. They got out of Somalia and kind of backed off of that for a time. And he's like, if we went over there and did what we had to do, which involved returning fire against people who were using women as human shields, you can't delete those files. This guy's got that in his brain for life, right? So he's normal. He wrestles with that. It's hard to say you had to participate in something where that was involved. And it was the right decision. He asked me, did I make the right decision? Absolutely you did. If you didn't return fire, your own unit would have been killed. Maybe you would have been killed. You had to return fire to break contact from them. And that was the right thing to do. Their own friends, family members, neighbors are the ones that put them at risk, not you. He wrestled with that. And he's like, if we were going to do all those things, why did we quit? Why did we go in the first place? And I think that's why you owe it to the people that are going to wage our nation's wars to at least vote. You're going to send them into harm's way. You're going to ask this of them. You should at least vote. And I think when you do vote, And Congress does its job, just like the founders intended, then the country is at war. And yeah, the countries that were either way, but it's pretty passive on behalf of Congress. So you ask this of people by continuing to cut the checks, but you want to ignore those tough things. And I think that's part of it. People don't want to be held accountable to account or have to wrestle with the tough decisions that people do on the battlefield. And my colleagues, if they're going to cut a check for that, They ought to have to vote on it.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The speaker believes the decision to return fire was morally correct.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "Some of the biggest critics of military engagement are individuals who did not serve in the military.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
