  {
    "episode_id": 84578,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84578.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 16,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, I'm grateful I had kind of given up hope on having the response. I was able to eventually have a conversation with Secretary Blinken that produced, I think, his response to this letter. And it's like, hey, I've been waiting for quite a while for an answer. And so we had a conversation and in short order after that, I did get the letter. And look, I don't agree with all that I think the State Department's implying with that response. And the part that's left unsaid, I mean, you know, you know, go back to, you know, I think Frederick Posty, that's the guy that what's seen versus unseen. And, you know, kind of the extension of that's what's said and what's unsaid is really NATO membership. I think that a lot of the US national security apparatus, a lot of NATO and therefore the European Union's national security apparatus sees it as is Ukraine part of NATO or not. And of course, that is a big part of why Russia felt that they were going to intervene. Now, there's nothing that excuses Putin's invasion. It was an unjust invasion of Ukraine. Nothing calls for that. Frankly, all kinds of things are just to repel it. But the question is, should America do those things? And right now, I think what's obvious to a lot of people is that Ukraine does not have the combat power of their own accord. Even if we cut big checks for them and they have technology and weapon systems, they do not have the manpower to extract Russia from all of Ukraine, especially all of Ukraine, including Crimea. So my hope is the part that is said is if we can create a framework that acknowledges these things, then there's a possibility that we would be open to a negotiated settlement. And at the end of the day, that decision really is Ukraine's. But it is also our decision as to what we're going to fund and not fund. Just as it was France's decision when they helped fund our own revolution and separation from you know, from Great Britain. You know, thankfully we got our own country and France was generous to help us. They weren't necessarily obligated to, but you know, we would have had a hard time winning without the support they gave us. And reality is Ukraine has done the fighting, but you know, we've done a lot of funding and we've done a lot of equipping as have some of our allies in Europe and around the world. And so hopefully Ukraine can make the most of that and create something that is defensible, and it has security, it has governance, and it has a strong economy. And if they can do that, then there's a chance for peace.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
