  {
    "episode_id": 84578,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84578.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 22,
    "turn_text": "Well, there is a healthy difference of opinion among Republican elected officials. You've got Neocon, Nikki Haley. I'm a never Nikki person for that reason and some others. And she would, of course, be ready for lots of wars in lots of places. just like her fellow South Carolinian, Lindsey Graham. And if somebody like that were in office, they would go about it very differently than Donald Trump would go about it. And we have the benefit of knowing how Donald Trump would go about it, because he went about it. He was very successful in peacemaking as president. He created enough uncertainty in our adversaries and rivals around the world to act as a deterrent. same kind of mantra as Reagan, peace through strength, supported a much stronger, more focused military. And because of that, he equipped Mike Pompeo to be a very effective Secretary of State in the diplomacy. And I would hope that we would see the same kinds of results from President Trump and maybe again Secretary of State Pompeo. I think it would be a much more effective approach. But it does highlight that the Republican Party is somewhat divided in which way we go. We still have a number of, you know, kind of neo-conservative more wars and more places kind of mindset that have been part of the party fundamentally since post 9-11. So prior to that, you know, we go back to the power doctrine. Republicans have broadly embraced a more limited involvement in wars. and even Dwight Eisenhower. Dwight Eisenhower was never accused of being an isolationist. He was a five-star general, led the war on D-Day that won World War II. But as president, he was much more limited in how he used force. He focused on America's infrastructure, not America's involvement in lots of wars in lots of places. He kept a strong deterrent as part of the Cold War, but he did it in a more responsible way. And because of that, we were able to pay down the national debt and invest in America. And I would hope that we go back to that era of Republican politics within our party.",
