  },
  {
    "episode_id": 84578,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84578.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 24,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, so the Patriot Act is probably the thing most people know is post 9-11. It basically said that the government's going to be able to spy on you, but don't worry, it's just to keep you safe. And a lot of people cautioned at the time, this is going to be abused. Frankly, it passed, I think, with 98 to one in the Senate and it passed with all but like 63 votes in the House. And so George Bush was president, but a lot of Democrats will say, oh, well, this was a Republican idea because it happened when George Bush was president. It was overwhelmingly bipartisan at the time. And there were Republicans and Democrats who objected in the House. I think actually more Democrats did object than Republicans. But I go back to Ron Paul, who was a member of the House at the time. He was one of the most vocal critics. He said, this is going to be used to spy against Americans. It will weaponize our government. And lo and behold, it has, just as he predicted it would. And frankly, just as our Founding Fathers would have predicted it would, that's why they had the separation of powers. The whole bill of rights was passed to be a limiting factor on what the federal government can do. And the fourth amendment is the one that says that you have an expectation of privacy. And the government's supposed to get a warrant or a subpoena to go after your information, not to spy on you. And a lot of people today are so used to how infringed their right to privacy is that they'll say, well, if you have nothing to hide, you have nothing to fear. Why are you so worried about this? And that's not what the Fourth Amendment says. It says you have a reasonable expectation of privacy. There's no asterisk beside it. And I think we have to defend our Constitution. And the right to privacy is core to the rights of American citizens. That the default setting is Yeah, don't hurt people don't take their stuff. And one of the ways that you're harmed is when people invade your privacy because it never stops there. They start using that invasion of privacy to limit speech or to limit your freedom to travel. You know, and we've seen that through most recently, COVID domestically. And we saw it in great measure in Canada as an example where people had their bank accounts frozen because they went to a trucker rally or something. Those same kinds of things, you know, in a different way, maybe are done here in the US. And let's go back to Edward Snowden, who kind of tried to blow the whistle on this. He didn't follow the right whistleblower laws. Part of where he went public with his information about the way the US government was spying on its own citizens is after the Director of National Intelligence clapper had testified before Congress and lied under oath to Congress and said, we don't do that. And Snowden said, oh, yes, you do. And here's all the other stuff you're doing. Now, the way he did that was irresponsible, was dangerous, and I think probably contributed to the compromise of some of our intelligence assets. So, you know, it's not a blank slate for Edward Snowden, but the reality is it shouldn't be a blank slate for our intelligence agencies either and shame on us if we don't limit their ability to spy on American citizens. Surely, at this point, we've learned from our mistakes.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The Patriot Act is widely recognized as a major post-9/11 policy.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "The Patriot Act granted the U.S. government expanded surveillance powers over individuals.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "The Fourth Amendment protects citizens' reasonable expectation of privacy and requires warrants for government access to personal information.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "The government claimed surveillance under the Patriot Act was justified by national security concerns.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Many people warned during the passage of the Patriot Act that it could lead to abuse.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "The Patriot Act passed with overwhelming bipartisan support in both the Senate and the House.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Ron Paul was a prominent critic of the Patriot Act during its passage and predicted it would enable government surveillance of Americans.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "The argument 'if you have nothing to hide, you have nothing to fear' misrepresents the spirit and letter of the Fourth Amendment.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Edward Snowden leaked classified information about U.S. surveillance programs after being misled by a false congressional testimony.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "Snowden’s methods were reckless and may have compromised intelligence operations, though the underlying revelations remain valid.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "The Framers of the Constitution intended the Bill of Rights to serve as a permanent check on governmental power.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "A healthy democracy depends on protecting civil liberties even in times of perceived crisis.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Government surveillance without proper oversight inevitably leads to abuse and erosion of individual freedoms.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "The legitimacy of constitutional protections does not depend on whether individuals believe they have anything to hide.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Public trust in institutions diminishes when officials lie under oath about surveillance practices.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Whistleblowing, while potentially risky, serves a necessary function in exposing systemic abuses within powerful state apparatuses.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "Infringements on privacy often extend beyond data collection to affect free speech and movement.",
        "confidence": 0.88
